            "type": "reasoning.text",
            "text": "We are asked: \"You are an expert in Sanskrit literature classification. For each Sanskrit work title provided in IAST, you need to: 1. Translate the title into Russian and English 2. Classify the work according to the traditional Sanskrit literary categories... Return JSON only with exactly this structure: { \"results\": [ { \"id\": \"uuid_string_from_input\", \"title_sa_iast\": \"original_title\", \"title_ru\": \"translation_in_russian\", \"title_en\": \"translation_in_english\", \"classification_codes\": [\"code1\", \"code2\"] } ] }\"\n\nWe have a list of works with IDs and IAST titles. Need to provide translations and classification codes for each.\n\nLet's process each work one by one.\n\nWork 1: id: a65ee37d-8e18-41ae-8fce-3275f5f73e51, title: Abhidharmakośa\n- Title translation: Abhidharmakośa means \"Treasury of Abhidharma\" (or \"Storehouse of Higher Doctrine\"). It's a key Buddhist text. English: \"Treasury of Abhidharma\". Russian: \"Сокровищница Абхидхармы\" (or \"Абхидхармакоша\" as is often used in Russian Indology). But we need to translate, not transliterate. So Russian: \"Сокровищница высшего учения\" or \"Сокровищница Абхидхармы\"? The term \"Abhidharma\" is often left untranslated. But the instruction says \"translate\", so we might try: \"Сокровищница высшей дхармы\" or simply \"Абхидхармакоша\" as a proper name. I'll use \"Сокровищница Абхидхармы\". English: \"Treasury of Abhidharma\".\n- Classification: It's a Buddhist philosophical treatise. Codes: buddhist. Possibly also a shastra, but from the list, \"buddhist\" is one. Also, it's a treatise on Abhidharma, which is part of Buddhist canon. Might also be classified as a kind of shastra, but we have \"buddhist\". So classification: [\"buddhist\"].\n\nWork 2: Abhidharmakośabhāṣya\n- This is a commentary on the above by Vasubandhu. Bhāṣya means commentary. Title: \"Commentary on the Treasury of Abhidharma\". English: \"Commentary on the Abhidharmakośa\". Russian: \"Комментарий к Сокровищнице Абхидхармы\". Or \"Абхидхармакошабхашья\". I'll translate as \"Комментарий на Абхидхармакошу\". But to be consistent: \"Комментарий к «Сокровищнице Абхидхармы»\". Classification: It's a commentary on a Buddhist text, so it's still Buddhist. And specifically it's a bhāṣya. So codes: [\"buddhist\", \"commentary\", \"bhashya\"]. From the list: \"commentary\" and \"bhashya\" are codes. So include both: [\"buddhist\", \"bhashya\", \"commentary\"]? Actually \"bhashya\" is a type of commentary. So it fits both. I'll include both.\n\nWork 3: Abhidhānacintāmaṇi\n- This is a lexicon by Hemachandra, a Jain author. \"Abhidhāna\" means \"designation, dictionary\", \"cintāmaṇi\" means \"wish-granting gem\". So \"Wish-granting gem of dictionaries\". English: \"Wish-granting Gem of Synonyms\" or \"Dictionary Cintamani\". Russian: \"Чинтамани словарей\" or \"Исполняющая желания драгоценность синонимов\". Actually it's a thesaurus. More precisely: \"Абхидханачинтамани (Драгоценный камень слов)\". I'll translate: \"Драгоценный камень синонимов\". English: \"The Wish-Fulfilling Gem of Synonyms\". Classification: It's a kosha (lexicon). Also it's by a Jain author, so could be \"jaina\" and \"kosha\". So codes: [\"jaina\", \"kosha\"]. Also \"nighantu\"? Actually nighantu is a glossary of Vedic words, while kosha is a general lexicon. Abhidhānacintāmaṇi is a kosha. So use \"kosha\".\n\nWork 4: Abhinavacintāmaṇi\n- Not sure. Possibly an Ayurvedic or alchemical text? \"Abhinava\" means \"new\", \"cintāmaṇi\" as before. Might be a work on medicine? I recall a text \"Abhinavacintāmaṇi\" in Ayurveda. Title: \"New Wish-granting Gem\". English: \"The New Cintāmaṇi\". Russian: \"Новая чинтамани\". Classification: Possibly \"ayurveda\" or \"rasashastra\"? I'm not sure. Might be a work on medicine. Quick check: There is an Ayurvedic text \"Abhinava Cintamani\"? Actually I think it's a Rasashastra text. Let's see: There is a famous alchemical text \"Rasārṇava\", but Abhinavacintāmaṇi could be a tantra or Ayurvedic. I'll search memory: I recall \"Abhinavacintāmaṇi\" is a Nighantu? No. Maybe it's a commentary? I'll guess it's an Ayurvedic text. But we can also leave it as \"ayurveda\" if uncertain. Alternatively, might be a stotra? I'd go with [\"ayurveda\"] tentatively. But let's be safe: maybe it's a \"tantra\"? No. I think it's a medical work. So [\"ayurveda\"].\n\nWork 5: Acintyastava\n- \"Acintya\" means \"inconceivable\", \"stava\" means \"hymn, praise\". So \"Hymn to the Inconceivable\". Likely a Buddhist stotra. There is a text by Aśvaghoṣa? Actually \"Acintyastava\" is a Buddhist hymn. English: \"Praise of the Inconceivable\". Russian: \"Хвала Непостижимому\". Classification: \"buddhist\", \"stotra\". So codes: [\"buddhist\", \"stotra\"].\n\nWork 6: Agastīyaratnaparīkṣā\n- \"Agastīya\" refers to the sage Agastya. \"Ratnaparīkṣā\" means \"examination of gems\". So \"Agastya's Examination of Gems\". This is a text on gemology. English: \"Agastya's Testing of Gems\". Russian: \"Агастья-ратнапарикша\" or \"Испытание драгоценных камней Агастьи\". Classification: Probably \"rasashastra\"? Or \"jyotisha\"? Actually gemology is part of traditional sciences. It might be classified under \"rasashastra\" or \"ayurveda\"? I'm not sure. There is a text \"Ratnapariksha\" attributed to Agastya. It's a scientific text. The classification list includes \"rasashastra\" which deals with alchemy and gems. So I'll use [\"rasashastra\"].\n\nWork 7: Agnipurāṇa\n- The Agni Purana is a Purana. English: \"The Fire Purana\". Russian: \"Агни-пурана\". Classification: \"purana\". So [\"purana\"].\n\nWork 8: Aitareya-Āraṇyaka\n- Aitareya Aranyaka, part of the Rigvedic tradition. English: \"Aitareya Aranyaka\". Russian: \"Айтарея-араньяка\". Classification: \"aranyaka\". Also it's related to Veda, but specifically aranyaka. So [\"aranyaka\"].\n\nWork 9: Aitareyabrāhmaṇa\n- Aitareya Brahmana, of Rigveda. English: \"Aitareya Brahmana\". Russian: \"Айтарея-брахмана\". Classification: \"brahmana\". So [\"brahmana\"].\n\nWork 10: Aitareyopaniṣad\n- Aitareya Upanishad, from Rigveda. English: \"Aitareya Upanishad\". Russian: \"Айтарея-упанишада\". Classification: \"upanishad\". So [\"upanishad\"].\n\nWork 11: Amarakośa\n- Amarakosha, the famous Sanskrit lexicon by Amarasimha. English: \"Amara's Dictionary\". Russian: \"Амаракоша (Словарь Амары)\". Classification: \"kosha\". So [\"kosha\"].\n\nWork 12: Amaraughaśāsana\n- I'm not familiar. \"Amara\" might be a name, \"ogha\" means \"flood, stream\", \"śāsana\" means \"teaching, instruction\". Possibly a Buddhist text? \"Amaraughaśāsana\" might be \"The Teaching of Amara\"? Not sure. Perhaps it's a commentary? Actually it might be a tantric text? I recall a text \"Amaraughaśāsana\" maybe a work by Advaita author? Quick search memory: Could be a Vedanta text? I think \"Amaraughaśāsana\" is a commentary on something. Given the name, it could be a \"śāsana\" which is a rule or teaching. Might be a \"dharmasutra\"? I'm not confident. Since the next work is \"Commentary on Amaraughaśāsana\", it suggests it's a root text. What kind of text? Possibly a \"tantra\" or \"agama\"? I'll guess it's a \"tantra\". Or maybe a \"stotra\"? Not sure. Let's look at the pattern: In the list we have \"Amaraughaśāsana\" and then \"Commentary on Amaraughaśāsana\". So it's a work that has a commentary. Could be a philosophical treatise. I'll check: There is a well-known text \"Amaraugha Śāsana\" maybe by Abhinavagupta? No. Actually, I think \"Amaraughaśāsana\" is a Buddhist work? I'm drawing blanks. Let's consider the name: \"Amara\" often means immortal, or could be a person. \"Ogha\" means rapid flow. Thus \"The teaching of the immortal stream\"? Might be a Buddhist tantra? I'd rather not guess wildly. Possibly a \"darshana\" text? But we have specific codes. It might be a \"buddhist\" or \"jaina\" text. I'll search my knowledge: There is a Jain text \"Amara-ghaśāsana\"? Not known. I think I'll assign it to \"buddhist\" as a guess, or perhaps \"agama\". But I should be consistent. Since it has a commentary, and it's called \"śāsana\", it might be a rule book. Maybe it's a \"dharmasutra\"? But then it would be Amara's Dharmasutra. Not standard. I'll go with [\"buddhist\"] tentatively. Or maybe \"tantra\"? I'll put [\"tantra\"]. But I'm not sure. I'll look at the list: there is \"commentary\" on it, so classification for the root text is whatever genre it is. I need to think if I know any \"Amaraughaśāsana\". I think it might be a text on yoga or something. I recall a text \"Amaraughaprabodha\"? No. Let's move on; I can put [\"tantra\"] for now, and possibly adjust later. I'll go with [\"tantra\"] because it sounds Tantric with \"ogha\".\nBut wait, \"śāsana\" is often used for the teachings of a Buddha or Tirthankara. So it could be \"buddhist\" or \"jaina\". I'll put [\"buddhist\"] because it's more common. But then again, \"Amara\" could be a Jain monk. I'll put [\"jaina\"]? Not sure. I'll stick with [\"buddhist\"] for now. Actually, a quick thought: There is a text \"Amaraughaśāsana\" in the context of Kashmir Shaivism? I think there is a work called \"Amaraughaśāsana\" by Gorakhnath? Possibly a Nath text. The Nath tradition is often considered Tantric. So [\"tantra\"] might be appropriate. Or \"yoga\". I'll go with [\"tantra\"].\n\nWork 13: Amaruśataka\n- The Amaruśataka is a collection of love lyrics by Amaru. English: \"Amaru's Hundred Verses\". Russian: \"Шатака Амару\". Classification: \"shataka\" (a collection of 100 verses). Also it's a kavya, specifically muktaka poetry. We have codes \"shataka\" and \"kavya\". I'll put [\"shataka\", \"kavya\"]. \"shataka\" is a type of minor kavya.\n\nWork 14: Amṛtabindūpaniṣat\n- Amritabindu Upanishad, a Yoga Upanishad. English: \"The Drop of Nectar Upanishad\". Russian: \"Амритабинду-упанишада\". Classification: \"upanishad\". So [\"upanishad\"].\n\nWork 15: Arthaśāstra\n- Kautilya's Arthashastra, treatise on statecraft and economics. English: \"Treatise on Statecraft\" or \"Science of Politics\". Russian: \"Артхашастра (Наука о пользе/политике)\". Classification: It's a shastra but from the list, what? Possibly \"smriti\"? Arthashastra is often classified as a part of the traditional sciences. But we have no direct code for \"arthashastra\". It might be considered a \"dharmasutra\" or \"smriti\"? Actually it's separate. The list includes \"smriti\", which covers law and dharma texts. Arthashastra is not exactly smriti. It could be considered a \"niti\" text. But we don't have that. I think the closest from the list is \"dharmasutra\" because it deals with duties of the king, but that's not accurate. Alternatively, we could consider it \"darshana\" as a school? No. Perhaps it's just \"shastra\" but not in list. The list has specific categories. I'll check if there's a category like \"nibandha\" or something. Actually, Arthashastra is a genre of its own. In Sanskrit classification, it might be grouped under \"smriti\" as a dharma-related text? I think traditionally, the fourteen vidyas include Arthashastra, but we need to pick from the given codes. The codes include \"dharmasutra\" and \"smriti\". I think Arthashastra is sometimes considered an upanga of Vedas? Actually, it's part of the sciences. The list does not have a separate \"arthashastra\" code. We could use \"smriti\" loosely because it's a treatise on social order. Or maybe we can use \"nibandha\"? No. I'll use [\"smriti\"] because Arthashastra is a smriti-like text on dharma of kings. But it's not exactly. Might also be considered a \"shastra\" but we don't have that generic code. Perhaps \"dharmasutra\"? I recall that Kautilya's Arthashastra is sometimes grouped with Dharmashastra. So I'll put [\"dharmasutra\"]? Actually dharmasutra is a specific genre of early prose texts on dharma. Arthashastra is not a dharmasutra. Maybe just [\"smriti\"]. I'll go with [\"smriti\"].\n\nWork 16: Atharvaprāyaścittāni\n- \"Atharva\" refers to Atharvaveda, \"prāyaścittāni\" means \"expiations\" or \"penances\". So \"Expiations of the Atharvaveda\". This is a text on rituals for expiation associated with the Atharvaveda. English: \"Atharvan Expiations\". Russian: \"Атхарва-праяшчиттани\". Classification: This is likely a pariśiṣṭa or appendix to Atharvaveda, dealing with prāyaścitta. It might be classified under \"kalpa\" or specifically \"śrautasūtra\"? Actually prāyaścitta is part of the kalpa tradition. Or it could be a \"smriti\" text. But from the list, we have \"kalpa\" for ritual. I think it's part of Atharvavedic ritual literature. I'll put [\"kalpa\"] or maybe [\"pariśiṣṭa\"]? But no pariśiṣṭa code. So [\"kalpa\"]. Also note \"Atharvavedapariśiṣṭa\" is a separate entry, so this might be part of it. So classification: [\"kalpa\"].\n\nWork 17: Atharvaveda (Paippalāda)\n- This is a recension of Atharvaveda. English: \"Atharvaveda (Paippalāda recension)\". Russian: \"Атхарваведа (Пайппалада)\". Classification: \"veda\", \"samhita\"? Actually Atharvaveda is a Veda, and its recension is a Samhita. So codes: [\"veda\", \"samhita\"]. We'll put both.\n\nWork 18: Atharvaveda (Śaunaka)\n- Similarly, another recension. So [\"veda\", \"samhita\"].\n\nWork 19: Atharvavedapariśiṣṭa\n- The Pariśiṣṭa (appendices) of Atharvaveda. This is a collection of auxiliary texts. English: \"Atharvaveda Appendices\". Russian: \"Атхарваведа-паришишта\". Classification: These texts cover various topics: rituals, omens, etc. It is regarded as a Vedāṅga? No, Pariśiṣṭa are like supplements. Might be classified under \"kalpa\" or \"jyotisha\"? It contains many things. But likely it is a \"pariśiṣṭa\" associated with Veda. Since we don't have a \"pariśiṣṭa\" code, I'll use [\"kalpa\"] as many are ritual. Alternatively, \"smriti\"? Not. I'll put [\"kalpa\"] as it's part of ritual literature.\n\nWork 20: Avadānaśataka\n- Avadānaśataka is a Buddhist collection of 100 avadānas (legends). English: \"The Hundred Avadānas\". Russian: \"Авадана-шатака\". Classification: \"buddhist\", \"katha\"? Avadānas are stories, so could be \"katha\". But specifically, the code for such a collection might be \"buddhist\" and maybe \"shastra\"? Better: [\"buddhist\", \"katha\"]. Or just \"buddhist\". But we have \"katha\" for stories. So both.\n\nWork 21: Ayurvedarasāyana\n- \"Rasāyana\" means alchemy/elixir, \"Ayurveda\" medicine. So \"The Rasāyana of Ayurveda\". Possibly a text on rejuvenation therapy. English: \"Ayurvedic Rasāyana\". Russian: \"Аюрведа-расаяна\". Classification: \"ayurveda\", \"rasashastra\". Both relevant.\n\nWork 22: Aṣṭasāhasrikā\n- Aṣṭasāhasrikā Prajñāpāramitā Sūtra, a Buddhist Mahayana sutra. \"Aṣṭasāhasrikā\" means \"the one with eight thousand [verses]\". Normally full title Aṣṭasāhasrikā Prajñāpāramitā. Here just Aṣṭasāhasrikā. English: \"The Perfection of Wisdom in Eight Thousand Lines\". Russian: \"Аштасахасрика (Восьмитысячная)\". Classification: \"buddhist\". Also it's a sutra, so maybe \"buddhist\" only. Code list has \"buddhist\".\n\nWork 23: Aṣṭādhyāyī\n- Panini's grammar, the Aṣṭādhyāyī (\"Eight Chapters\"). English: \"Eight Chapters\". Russian: \"Аштадхьяи (Восьмикнижие)\". Classification: \"vyakarana\". So [\"vyakarana\"].\n\nWork 24: Aṣṭāvakragīta\n- Aṣṭāvakra Gita, a Vedantic text. English: \"Song of Ashtavakra\". Russian: \"Аштавакра-гита\". Classification: It's a dialogue on Advaita Vedanta. So codes: \"vedanta\", \"darshana\". Also could be \"smriti\"? It's often grouped with the Gita. I'll put [\"vedanta\"]. Also maybe \"stotra\"? It's a gita. Not exactly stotra. So [\"vedanta\"] is fine.\n\nWork 25: Aṣṭāṅgahṛdayasaṃhitā\n- This is an Ayurvedic text, the heart of the eight branches of Ayurveda. English: \"Heart of the Eightfold Medicine\". Russian: \"Аштанга-хридая-самхита\". Classification: \"ayurveda\". It's a samhita, but the code \"samhita\" is for Vedic samhitas. Here it's a medical samhita. So [\"ayurveda\"] only.\n\nWork 26: Aṣṭāṅganighaṇṭu\n- An Ayurvedic lexicon of medicinal plants? \"Aṣṭāṅga\" refers to the eight branches, \"nighaṇṭu\" is a glossary. So \"Nighaṇṭu of the Eight Branches\". English: \"Eight-limbed Nighantu\". Russian: \"Аштанга-нигханту\". Classification: \"nighantu\", \"ayurveda\". So both.\n\nWork 27: Aṣṭāṅgasaṃgraha\n- An Ayurvedic compendium by Vāgbhaṭa. English: \"Compendium of the Eight Branches\". Russian: \"Аштанга-санграха\". Classification: \"ayurveda\".\n\nWork 28: Baudhāyanadharmasūtra\n- The Dharmasūtra of Baudhāyana. English: \"Baudhayana Dharmasutra\". Russian: \"Баудхаяна-дхармасутра\". Classification: \"dharmasutra\". Also part of the Kalpa? Actually dharmasutra is a subcategory of kalpa. But we have separate codes: \"kalpa\", \"dharmasutra\". I'll put [\"dharmasutra\", \"kalpa\"]? Perhaps just [\"dharmasutra\"] is enough, but since it's part of the Baudhayana Kalpasutra, and we have \"kalpa\", maybe both. But the instruction says provide the classification codes that best describe. I'll use [\"dharmasutra\"].\n\nWork 29: Baudhāyanagṛhyasūtra\n- The Gṛhyasūtra of Baudhāyana. English: \"Baudhayana Grihyasutra\". Russian: \"Баудхаяна-грихьясутра\". Classification: \"grihyasutra\", \"kalpa\". I'll put [\"grihyasutra\"].\n\nWork 30: Baudhāyanaśrautasūtra\n- The Śrautasūtra of Baudhāyana. English: \"Baudhayana Shrautasutra\". Russian: \"Баудхаяна-шраутасутра\". Classification: \"shrautasutra\", \"kalpa\". I'll put [\"shrautasutra\"].\n\nWork 31: Bhadrabāhucarita\n- \"Carita\" means life or deeds. Bhadrabāhu was a Jain sage. So \"Life of Bhadrabahu\". English: \"The Life of Bhadrabahu\". Russian: \"Бхадрабаху-чарита\". Classification: \"jaina\", \"kavya\"? Carita is a genre of poem. It could be a \"mahakavya\" or just \"kavya\". I'll put [\"jaina\", \"kavya\"].\n\nWork 32: Bhairavastava\n- Hymn to Bhairava, a Shaiva deity. English: \"Hymn to Bhairava\". Russian: \"Бхайрава-става\". Classification: \"stotra\", possibly \"tantra\"? It's a stotra. So [\"stotra\"].\n\nWork 33: Bhallaṭaśataka\n- The Bhallaṭaśataka is a collection of 100 verses by the poet Bhallaṭa. English: \"Bhallaṭa's Hundred Verses\". Russian: \"Бхаллата-шатака\". Classification: \"shataka\", \"kavya\". So [\"shataka\", \"kavya\"].\n\nWork 34: Bhramarāṣṭaka\n- An \"aṣṭaka\" is an eight-verse poem. \"Bhramara\" means \"bee\". So \"The Eight Verses on the Bee\". It might be a devotional poem to Krishna? I think there's a Bhramarāṣṭaka by Vallabhacharya? It's a stotra. English: \"Eight Verses on the Bee\". Russian: \"Бхрамараштака\". Classification: \"stotra\". Also maybe \"kavya\"? It's a lyrical poem. I'll put [\"stotra\"].\n\nWork 35: Bhāgavatapurāṇa\n- The Bhagavata Purana. English: \"The Bhagavata Purana\". Russian: \"Бхагавата-пурана\". Classification: \"purana\". So [\"purana\"].\n\nWork 36: Bhāradvājagṛhyasūtra\n- Gṛhyasūtra of Bhāradvāja. English: \"Bharadvaja Grihyasutra\". Russian: \"Бхарадваджа-грихьясутра\". Classification: \"grihyasutra\".\n\nWork 37: Bhāradvājaśrautasūtra\n- Śrautasūtra of Bhāradvāja. English: \"Bharadvaja Shrautasutra\". Russian: \"Бхарадваджа-шраутасутра\". Classification: \"shrautasutra\".\n\nWork 38: Bhāratamañjarī\n- A poem summarizing the Mahabharata, by Kṣemendra. English: \"The Cluster of Flowers of the Bharata\". Russian: \"Бхарата-манджари\". Classification: \"kavya\", possibly \"itihasa\"? It's based on itihasa, but it's a kavya. So [\"kavya\"].\n\nWork 39: Bhāvaprakāśa\n- An Ayurvedic text by Bhāvamiśra. English: \"Light of Existence\" or \"Illumination of Nature\". Russian: \"Бхава-пракаша\". Classification: \"ayurveda\".\n\nWork 40: Bodhicaryāvatāra\n- A key Mahayana Buddhist text by Śāntideva. \"Entering the Path of Enlightenment\" or \"A Guide to the Bodhisattva's Way of Life\". English: \"Introduction to the Bodhisattva's Way of Life\". Russian: \"Бодхичарья-аватара\". Classification: \"buddhist\". Also it's a philosophical treatise, so maybe \"darshana\"? But we have \"buddhist\".\n\nWork 41: Brahmabindūpaniṣat\n- Another Yoga Upanishad. English: \"Upanishad of the Brahman-point\". Russian: \"Брахмабинду-упанишада\". Classification: \"upanishad\".\n\nWork 42: Buddhacarita\n- Aśvaghoṣa's epic poem on the life of the Buddha. English: \"Acts of the Buddha\". Russian: \"Буддачарита\". Classification: It's a kavya, specifically a mahakavya. Codes: \"buddhist\", \"kavya\", \"mahakavya\". I'll put [\"buddhist\", \"mahakavya\"] because it's a great poem.\n\nWork 43: Bījanighaṇṭu\n- \"Bīja\" means seed, so a nighaṇṭu of seeds, likely an Ayurvedic glossary of seeds and plants. English: \"Seed Nighantu\". Russian: \"Биджа-нигханту\". Classification: \"nighantu\", \"ayurveda\". So both.\n\nWork 44: Bṛhadāraṇyakopaniṣad\n- The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad. English: \"Great Forest Upanishad\". Russian: \"Брихадараньяка-упанишада\". Classification: \"upanishad\". Also it's part of Shatapatha Brahmana, so an aranyaka? Actually it's an upanishad within an aranyaka. But primary classification is upanishad.\n\nWork 45: Bṛhatkathāślokasaṃgraha\n- A collection of verses from the Brihatkatha, by Buddhasvāmin. English: \"Collection of Verses of the Great Story\". Russian: \"Брихаткатха-шлока-санграха\". Classification: \"kavya\", \"katha\"? It's a story literature. I'll put [\"katha\", \"kavya\"].\n\nWork 46: Carakasaṃhitā\n- The Charaka Samhita, an Ayurvedic text. English: \"Charaka's Compendium\". Russian: \"Чарака-самхита\". Classification: \"ayurveda\". It's a samhita of medicine.\n\nWork 47: Carakatattvapradīpikā\n- A commentary on Charaka Samhita? \"Tattvapradīpikā\" means \"Lamp of Truths\". So \"Illumination of the Truths of Charaka\". Probably a commentary. English: \"Lamp of Charaka's Truths\". Russian: \"Чарака-таттва-прадипика\". Classification: \"commentary\", \"ayurveda\". So [\"commentary\", \"ayurveda\"].\n\nWork 48: Caurapañcaśikā\n- \"Fifty Verses of the Thief\" by Bilhana. English: \"The Thief's Fifty Verses\". Russian: \"Чаура-панчашика\". Classification: \"kavya\", \"shataka\"? It's a love poem of 50 verses. Technically a shataka is 100, but here 50. Could be classified as \"kavya\". Also maybe \"dutakavya\"? No, that's messenger poem. So [\"kavya\"].\n\nWork 49: Chāndogyopaniṣad\n- Chandogya Upanishad. English: \"Chandogya Upanishad\". Russian: \"Чхандогья-упанишада\". Classification: \"upanishad\".\n\nWork 50: Commentary on Amaraughaśāsana\n- Given the title, this is a commentary on the previously listed work. So it inherits the classification from the root text, plus \"commentary\". So codes: [\"commentary\"] plus whatever we assigned to Amaraughaśāsana. I earlier tentatively put [\"tantra\"] for Amaraughaśāsana. So for this commentary, codes: [\"tantra\", \"commentary\"]. But if I change my guess on Amaraughaśāsana, I need consistency. Let's reconsider: \"Amaraughaśāsana\" might be a text on Vedanta? I recall there is a Vedantic text \"Amaraughaśāsana\" by a certain author? Actually, I think it's a work on alchemy? \"Amara\" could be \"immortal\" and \"ogha\" stream, and \"śāsana\" teaching, so \"Teaching of the Immortal Stream\" could be a tantric text. I'll stick with [\"tantra\"] for root, so commentary is [\"tantra\", \"commentary\"]. But wait, the list has \"commentary\" as a code. There's no separate \"bhashya\" or \"tika\" code, but we can use \"commentary\" as a generic. Since it's a commentary, I'll just put [\"commentary\"] and the root text's classification. I'll also include \"bhashya\" if appropriate, but we don't know if it's a bhashya. I'll just use \"commentary\".\n\nNow let's assemble the JSON.\n\nI need to create for each work an object in \"results\" array. There are 50 works. I'll list them with the translations and codes.\n\nFor Russian translations, I'll try to provide accurate translations. Some titles are often left untransliterated in Russian, but I'll provide the translation. For example, for well-known titles like \"Abhidharmakośa\", I might give \"Абхидхармакоша\" but that's transliteration. The instruction says \"translate\", so I'll try to 